1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,987
This is ECE 320, video 20. The topic is dynamic scheduling, part 1: the scoreboard. It is unit 11 of the slides.

2
00:00:11,437 --> 00:00:19,031
After this video, you can do four things. One: explain why an in-order pipeline stalls more than it must.

3
00:00:19,481 --> 00:00:24,879
Two: explain the instruction buffer, and the two new stages, dispatch and issue.

4
00:00:25,329 --> 00:00:29,147
Three: fill the three scoreboard tables, cycle by cycle.

5
00:00:29,597 --> 00:00:35,976
Four: find the structural, RAW, WAW and WAR stalls of a scoreboard.

6
00:00:36,426 --> 00:00:42,314
The basic pipeline started with single, in-order issue and single-cycle operations.

7
00:00:42,764 --> 00:00:46,049
We extended it with multi-cycle operations.

8
00:00:46,499 --> 00:00:50,617
Then with multiple issue. This is the superscalar pipeline.

9
00:00:51,067 --> 00:00:56,101
Now: dynamic scheduling. Instructions issue and execute out of order.

10
00:00:56,551 --> 00:01:05,021
The first method is the scoreboard. It executes out of order, but it does not solve WAW and WAR hazards.

11
00:01:05,471 --> 00:01:14,324
The second method is Tomasulo's algorithm. It adds register renaming to fix WAR and WAW hazards.

12
00:01:14,774 --> 00:01:19,425
The slides of part 1 teach the scoreboard. This video follows them.

13
00:01:19,875 --> 00:01:25,656
The next half unit adds precise state and speculation, and dynamic load scheduling.

14
00:01:26,106 --> 00:01:41,807
Here is an in-order pipeline with a multi-cycle execute stage. Three floating-point instructions run: divide, add and multiply. Cycle 1: divf is fetched. Cycle 2: divf decodes, and addf is fetched.

15
00:01:42,257 --> 00:01:56,167
Cycles 3 to 6: divf executes for 4 cycles. The slash marks the divider. addf stays in decode, with a star. A star means a stall. mulf stays in fetch, also with a star.

16
00:01:56,617 --> 00:02:09,438
Cycles 7 to 11: divf writes back in cycle 7. addf executes in cycles 7 and 8. mulf executes in cycles 9 and 10, and writes back in cycle 11.

17
00:02:09,888 --> 00:02:24,757
Why does addf stall in cycle 3? addf reads f0, and divf writes f0 in cycle 7. This is a RAW hazard. It is a fundamental problem: addf must wait for its operand.

18
00:02:25,207 --> 00:02:33,335
Why does mulf stall in cycle 3? mulf uses only f2 and f4. It does not need the result of divf.

19
00:02:33,785 --> 00:02:43,151
mulf cannot go into decode, because addf is there. Each stage holds one instruction. That is the only reason. It is not good enough.

20
00:02:43,601 --> 00:02:54,886
The idea of slide 4: decode mulf in cycle 4 and execute it in cycle 5. No fundamental reason stops this. Dynamic scheduling does this.

21
00:02:55,336 --> 00:03:04,531
Dynamic scheduling is also called out-of-order execution. The program gives the instructions in an order: divf, addf, mulf.

22
00:03:04,981 --> 00:03:15,626
The hardware can execute them in another order: divf, mulf, addf. This is a non-sequential order. The slides call it non-von Neumann order.

23
00:03:16,076 --> 00:03:18,145
The first gain: fewer stalls.

24
00:03:18,595 --> 00:03:22,606
The second gain: the functional units are busy more often.

25
00:03:23,056 --> 00:03:30,117
The third gain: parallel execution. When the order is not fixed, instructions can run in parallel.

26
00:03:30,567 --> 00:03:38,247
But the result must look like sequential execution. This is extremely important, and it is hard to get correct.

27
00:03:38,697 --> 00:03:43,625
Scheduling means: re-arrange the instructions to get the best performance.

28
00:03:44,075 --> 00:03:50,177
To schedule, you must know the structure of the processor, the latencies, and the dependencies.

29
00:03:50,627 --> 00:03:56,195
Two options. Dynamic scheduling: the hardware schedules. This unit is about it.

30
00:03:56,645 --> 00:03:59,546
Static scheduling: the compiler schedules.

31
00:03:59,996 --> 00:04:06,439
The compiler works at compile time, before the program runs. The hardware works at run time.

32
00:04:06,889 --> 00:04:14,675
Why build complex hardware? One: performance portability. You do not want to recompile for each new machine.

33
00:04:15,125 --> 00:04:25,664
Two: the hardware has more information. Addresses, branch directions and cache misses are known only at run time. The compiler does not know them.

34
00:04:26,114 --> 00:04:36,781
Three: the hardware has more resources. The compiler may not have enough architectural registers to fix WAR and WAW hazards.

35
00:04:37,231 --> 00:04:43,460
Four: it is easier to speculate in hardware. It is easier to recover from a mis-speculation.

36
00:04:43,910 --> 00:04:46,577
But the compiler can look at more instructions.

37
00:04:47,027 --> 00:04:53,661
So a combination is possible. The compiler does as much as it can, and the hardware does the rest.

38
00:04:54,111 --> 00:05:07,935
Here is a simple in-order pipeline with 4 stages: IF, ID, EX and WB. EX takes multiple cycles and includes the memory access. Each red bar is a pipeline register.

39
00:05:08,385 --> 00:05:20,375
Each stage has one pipeline register. So each stage holds one instruction per cycle. This is a structural hazard. Here the older instruction is stalled in ID.

40
00:05:20,825 --> 00:05:27,651
The younger instruction cannot pass the older one. It could pass only if it killed the older instruction.

41
00:05:28,101 --> 00:05:34,096
An out-of-order pipeline adds this function: a younger instruction can pass an older one.

42
00:05:34,546 --> 00:05:41,181
The trick is an instruction buffer. It has many names. It is a set of latches that hold instructions.

43
00:05:41,631 --> 00:05:47,881
The buffer is the scope of the scheduler: the scheduler can see only the instructions in the buffer.

44
00:05:48,331 --> 00:05:58,891
Split ID into two parts, ID1 and ID2. ID1 puts decoded instructions into the buffer in order. Here is instruction 1.

45
00:05:59,341 --> 00:06:00,963
Then instruction 2.

46
00:06:01,413 --> 00:06:05,551
Then instructions 3 and 4. They go in, in program order.

47
00:06:06,001 --> 00:06:14,407
ID2 sends instructions from the buffer down the rest of the pipeline out of order. Here instruction 3 goes first.

48
00:06:14,857 --> 00:06:25,843
The two parts of ID get names. Dispatch, D S, is the first part. It puts the instruction into the instruction buffer: it allocates resources there.

49
00:06:26,293 --> 00:06:31,307
This adds a new kind of structural hazard: the instruction buffer can be full.

50
00:06:31,757 --> 00:06:37,581
Dispatch is in order. When dispatch stalls, the stall goes to all younger instructions.

51
00:06:38,031 --> 00:06:44,815
Issue, I S, is the second part. It sends instructions from the buffer to the execution units.

52
00:06:45,265 --> 00:06:52,070
Issue is out of order. When one instruction stalls in issue, younger instructions can still issue.

53
00:06:52,520 --> 00:07:07,133
Dispatch method 1 is scoreboarding. The instruction buffer becomes the scoreboard. It is a centralized control scheme. It has no bypassing, and it does not remove WAR and WAW hazards.

54
00:07:07,583 --> 00:07:26,314
The first scoreboard was in the CDC 6600, in 1964. It had 16 separate functional units, not pipelined: 4 for floating point, 5 for memory, and 7 for integers. 4 plus 5 plus 7 is 16.

55
00:07:26,764 --> 00:07:39,052
Our example is the Simple Scoreboard. It has 5 functional units: one ALU, one load unit, one store unit, and two floating-point units, FP1 and FP2.

56
00:07:39,502 --> 00:07:46,777
All units take 1 cycle, except the floating-point units. They take 3 cycles, and they are pipelined.

57
00:07:47,227 --> 00:07:55,141
To keep it simple, the pipeline is 1-wide: not superscalar. The same ideas apply to wider pipelines.

58
00:07:55,591 --> 00:08:11,165
The scoreboard has three tables. Instruction status has one entry for each active instruction. It shows the stage of the instruction. An instruction in the scoreboard has dispatched. Here mulf dispatched in cycle 2.

59
00:08:11,615 --> 00:08:20,596
Functional unit status has one entry for each functional unit. Busy tells if the unit is busy. op is the current operation.

60
00:08:21,046 --> 00:08:26,870
R is the destination register. R1 and R2 are the source registers.

61
00:08:27,320 --> 00:08:33,677
T1 and T2 are the tags of the functional units that will produce the source registers.

62
00:08:34,127 --> 00:08:43,087
T is the tag of the functional unit that produces the destination register. It is the unit of this entry: here FP1.

63
00:08:43,537 --> 00:08:58,727
Register status has one entry for each architectural register. Its T is the tag of the functional unit, if any, that will write the register. Here load will write f0, and FP1 will write f4.

64
00:08:59,177 --> 00:09:11,251
The tag fields work as ready bits. A tag that is not empty means: the value is not ready in the register file. Here T1 is load, so f0 is not ready.

65
00:09:11,701 --> 00:09:19,339
An empty tag means: the value is ready in the register file. Here T2 is empty, so f2 is ready.

66
00:09:19,789 --> 00:09:35,789
Here is the Simple Scoreboard as hardware. Fetched instructions come in from the left. The scoreboard keeps instruction fields and status bits: the instruction status, and the functional unit status with R, R1 and R2.

67
00:09:36,239 --> 00:09:44,111
It keeps tags, in red: T, T1 and T2, the register status, and a comparator that compares tags.

68
00:09:44,561 --> 00:09:51,899
The values, in blue, are in the register file and the functional unit. The scoreboard holds no values.

69
00:09:52,349 --> 00:09:59,816
The new pipeline has five stages: IF, DS, IS, EX and WB. IF fetches the instruction.

70
00:10:00,266 --> 00:10:14,090
DS, dispatch, sends the instruction from fetch to the scoreboard. If there is a structural hazard or a WAW hazard, dispatch stalls. Otherwise it allocates a scoreboard entry.

71
00:10:14,540 --> 00:10:26,913
IS, issue, sends the instruction to its functional unit. If there is a RAW hazard, issue stalls. Otherwise it reads the registers and goes directly to execute.

72
00:10:27,363 --> 00:10:32,121
EX executes the operation. It tells the scoreboard when it is done.

73
00:10:32,571 --> 00:10:42,576
WB, write-back. If there is a WAR hazard, write-back stalls. Otherwise it writes the register and frees the scoreboard entry.

74
00:10:43,026 --> 00:10:54,375
Two assumptions. Write-back and a RAW-dependent issue can be in the same cycle. Write-back and a structural-dependent dispatch can also be in the same cycle.

75
00:10:54,825 --> 00:11:11,252
Dispatch, with the example of cycle 2. mulf f4, f0, f2 dispatches. FP1 is free, and the register status of f4 is empty. So there is no structural hazard and no WAW hazard.

76
00:11:11,702 --> 00:11:22,859
Dispatch allocates the scoreboard entry of FP1. It writes R, R1 and R2: f4, f0 and f2. T is FP1.

77
00:11:23,309 --> 00:11:37,155
Then it copies the status of the input registers. R1 is f0. The register status of f0 is load, because the load unit will write f0. So T1 becomes load.

78
00:11:37,605 --> 00:11:45,647
R2 is f2. The register status of f2 is empty. So T2 stays empty: f2 is ready.

79
00:11:46,097 --> 00:11:56,956
Then it sets the status of the output register. R is f4. The register status of f4 becomes FP1: FP1 will write f4.

80
00:11:57,406 --> 00:12:15,283
Issue, with the example of cycle 4. Issue waits for RAW hazards: it waits while T1 or T2 is not empty. In cycle 4 the load unit wrote f0, so T1 of FP1 is empty. T2 is also empty.

81
00:12:15,733 --> 00:12:20,789
So mulf issues in cycle 4. The instruction status gets the issue bit.

82
00:12:21,239 --> 00:12:31,458
Issue reads the registers. R1 and R2 select f0 and f2. Their values go from the register file to the functional unit.

83
00:12:31,908 --> 00:12:44,239
Execute, with the example. mulf executes in FP1 in cycles 5, 6 and 7. The instruction status shows 5 plus. The plus means more than one cycle.

84
00:12:44,689 --> 00:12:54,011
The tag T, FP1, goes with the operation to the functional unit. When the unit is done, it tells the scoreboard with this tag.

85
00:12:54,461 --> 00:13:10,568
Write-back, with the example of cycle 8. Write-back waits for WAR hazards: an older instruction that still must read the register. No older instruction waits to read f4. The store reads f4, but it is younger.

86
00:13:11,018 --> 00:13:16,949
So mulf writes back in cycle 8. The result goes into f4 in the register file.

87
00:13:17,399 --> 00:13:28,812
Then the scoreboard compares the tag FP1 with T1 and T2 of all waiting instructions. T1 of the store entry is FP1. It matches.

88
00:13:29,262 --> 00:13:43,747
On a match, clear the tag: the input is now ready. T1 of the store becomes empty. The register status of f4 is also cleared. The FP1 entry is freed: its fields become empty.

89
00:13:44,197 --> 00:13:53,243
The example is SAX, Scalar Alpha X. It is a simplified SAXPY. For each I, Z of I is A times X of I.

90
00:13:53,693 --> 00:14:10,013
Here is the assembly code. Load float loads X of i into f0. Multiply float puts A times f0 into f4. A is in f2. Store float stores f4 into Z of i. i is in x1.

91
00:14:10,463 --> 00:14:20,191
Add immediate adds 4 to x1. The branch loops while x2 is greater than or equal to x1. x2 holds 4 times N.

92
00:14:20,641 --> 00:14:27,958
We look at two iterations: the loop is unrolled, and the branch is ignored. So there are seven instructions.

93
00:14:28,408 --> 00:14:37,219
Instructions 5, 6 and 7 are the second iteration. They use the same registers as instructions 1, 2 and 3.

94
00:14:37,669 --> 00:14:52,261
Here are the tables for the example. Instruction status has one row for each of the seven instructions, and a column for each stage: DS, IS, EX and WB. Each cell will hold a cycle number.

95
00:14:52,711 --> 00:15:01,991
Functional unit status has one row for each of the five units: ALU, load, store, FP1 and FP2. All are free.

96
00:15:02,441 --> 00:15:10,825
Register status shows the four registers of the example: f0, f2, f4 and x1. All are ready.

97
00:15:11,275 --> 00:15:22,304
Remember: the ALU, load and store units take 1 cycle. FP1 and FP2 take 3 cycles. One instruction dispatches per cycle, in order.

98
00:15:22,754 --> 00:15:27,597
Cycle 1. All tables are empty: no instruction has dispatched.

99
00:15:28,047 --> 00:15:43,855
Load float, ldf f0,X(x1), dispatches in cycle 1. The load unit is free, and the register status of f 0 is empty. So there is no structural hazard and no WAW hazard.

100
00:15:44,305 --> 00:15:51,558
Allocate the load entry. Busy is yes, op is load float. R is f 0, R2 is x 1.

101
00:15:52,008 --> 00:15:58,728
Copy the status of the inputs. X 1 has no tag, so T2 stays empty: x 1 is ready.

102
00:15:59,178 --> 00:16:06,666
Set the status of the output. load will write f 0, so the register status of f 0 becomes load.

103
00:16:07,116 --> 00:16:11,489
Cycle 2. The tables show the state at the end of cycle 1.

104
00:16:11,939 --> 00:16:23,566
Load float, ldf f0,X(x1), issues in cycle 2. T1 and T2 of load are empty, so it reads x 1 from the register file.

105
00:16:24,016 --> 00:16:41,125
Multiply float, mulf f4,f0,f2, dispatches in cycle 2. The F P 1 unit is free, and the register status of f 4 is empty. So there is no structural hazard and no WAW hazard.

106
00:16:41,575 --> 00:16:51,751
Allocate the F P 1 entry. Busy is yes, op is multiply float. R is f 4, R1 is f 0, R2 is f 2.

107
00:16:52,201 --> 00:17:04,297
Copy the status of the inputs. The register status of f 0 is load, so T1 becomes load. F 2 has no tag, so T2 stays empty: f 2 is ready.

108
00:17:04,747 --> 00:17:13,089
Set the status of the output. F P 1 will write f 4, so the register status of f 4 becomes F P 1.

109
00:17:13,539 --> 00:17:17,891
Cycle 3. The tables show the state at the end of cycle 2.

110
00:17:18,341 --> 00:17:28,687
Multiply float in F P 1 cannot issue. Its T1 is load: f 0 is not ready. This is a RAW hazard, so it waits.

111
00:17:29,137 --> 00:17:31,804
Load float executes in cycle 3.

112
00:17:32,254 --> 00:17:44,286
Store float, stf f4,Z(x1), dispatches in cycle 3. The store unit is free, so there is no structural hazard. A store has no destination.

113
00:17:44,736 --> 00:17:52,309
Allocate the store entry. Busy is yes, op is store float. R1 is f 4, R2 is x 1.

114
00:17:52,759 --> 00:18:06,477
Copy the status of the inputs. The register status of f 4 is F P 1, so T1 becomes F P 1. X 1 has no tag, so T2 stays empty: x 1 is ready.

115
00:18:06,927 --> 00:18:11,321
Cycle 4. The tables show the state at the end of cycle 3.

116
00:18:11,771 --> 00:18:27,601
Load float, ldf f0,X(x1), writes back in cycle 4. No older instruction still waits to read f 0, so there is no WAR hazard. It writes f 0 into the register file.

117
00:18:28,051 --> 00:18:35,325
The register status of f 0 holds load. Clear it: f 0 is now ready in the register file.

118
00:18:35,775 --> 00:18:47,061
Compare the tag load with T1 and T2 of the waiting entries. T1 of F P 1 matches. Clear it: f 0 is now ready for F P 1.

119
00:18:47,511 --> 00:18:50,305
Free the load entry. Busy becomes no.

120
00:18:50,755 --> 00:19:11,790
Multiply float, mulf f4,f0,f2, issues in cycle 4. T1 and T2 of F P 1 are empty, so it reads f 0 and f 2 from the register file. The write-back in this cycle cleared its tag. A RAW-dependent issue can be in the same cycle.

121
00:19:12,240 --> 00:19:22,160
Store float in store cannot issue. Its T1 is F P 1: f 4 is not ready. This is a RAW hazard, so it waits.

122
00:19:22,610 --> 00:19:38,397
Add immediate, addi x1,x1,#4, dispatches in cycle 4. The A L U unit is free, and the register status of x 1 is empty. So there is no structural hazard and no WAW hazard.

123
00:19:38,847 --> 00:19:46,569
Allocate the A L U entry. Busy is yes, op is add immediate. R is x 1, R1 is x 1.

124
00:19:47,019 --> 00:19:53,697
Copy the status of the inputs. X 1 has no tag, so T1 stays empty: x 1 is ready.

125
00:19:54,147 --> 00:20:02,104
Set the status of the output. A L U will write x 1, so the register status of x 1 becomes A L U.

126
00:20:02,554 --> 00:20:07,013
Cycle 5. The tables show the state at the end of cycle 4.

127
00:20:07,463 --> 00:20:19,345
Add immediate, addi x1,x1,#4, issues in cycle 5. T1 and T2 of A L U are empty, so it reads x 1 from the register file.

128
00:20:19,795 --> 00:20:24,382
Store float in store still waits: its T1 is still F P 1.

129
00:20:24,832 --> 00:20:35,200
Multiply float starts to execute in cycle 5. FP1 takes 3 cycles: 5, 6 and 7. The table shows 5 plus.

130
00:20:35,650 --> 00:20:51,629
Load float, ldf f0,X(x1), dispatches in cycle 5. The load unit is free, and the register status of f 0 is empty. So there is no structural hazard and no WAW hazard.

131
00:20:52,079 --> 00:20:59,332
Allocate the load entry. Busy is yes, op is load float. R is f 0, R2 is x 1.

132
00:20:59,782 --> 00:21:07,398
Copy the status of the inputs. The register status of x 1 is A L U, so T2 becomes A L U.

133
00:21:07,848 --> 00:21:15,336
Set the status of the output. load will write f 0, so the register status of f 0 becomes load.

134
00:21:15,786 --> 00:21:20,159
Cycle 6. The tables show the state at the end of cycle 5.

135
00:21:20,609 --> 00:21:25,196
Store float in store still waits: its T1 is still F P 1.

136
00:21:25,646 --> 00:21:35,182
Load float in load cannot issue. Its T2 is A L U: x 1 is not ready. This is a RAW hazard, so it waits.

137
00:21:35,632 --> 00:21:38,448
Add immediate executes in cycle 6.

138
00:21:38,898 --> 00:21:58,973
mulf f4,f0,f2 cannot dispatch. It writes f 4, and the register status of f 4 holds F P 1. This is a WAW hazard. FP2 is free, so it is not a structural hazard. Dispatch is in order, so younger instructions wait too.

139
00:21:59,423 --> 00:22:03,775
Cycle 7. The tables show the state at the end of cycle 6.

140
00:22:04,225 --> 00:22:24,107
Add immediate finished executing in cycle 6, but it cannot write back x 1. The older stf f4,Z(x1) has not issued, and it reads x 1: its R2. Writing now would destroy the value it must read. This is a WAR hazard, so write-back stalls.

141
00:22:24,557 --> 00:22:29,144
Store float in store still waits: its T1 is still F P 1.

142
00:22:29,594 --> 00:22:33,946
Load float in load still waits: its T2 is still A L U.

143
00:22:34,396 --> 00:22:45,127
mulf f4,f0,f2 still cannot dispatch: the register status of f 4 still holds F P 1. WAW hazard.

144
00:22:45,577 --> 00:22:49,907
Cycle 8. The tables show the state at the end of cycle 7.

145
00:22:50,357 --> 00:23:05,909
Multiply float, mulf f4,f0,f2, writes back in cycle 8. No older instruction still waits to read f 4, so there is no WAR hazard. It writes f 4 into the register file.

146
00:23:06,359 --> 00:23:13,783
The register status of f 4 holds F P 1. Clear it: f 4 is now ready in the register file.

147
00:23:14,233 --> 00:23:24,601
Compare the tag F P 1 with T1 and T2 of the waiting entries. T1 of store matches. Clear it: f 4 is now ready for store.

148
00:23:25,051 --> 00:23:28,443
Free the F P 1 entry. Busy becomes no.

149
00:23:28,893 --> 00:23:48,776
Add immediate finished executing in cycle 6, but it cannot write back x 1. The older stf f4,Z(x1) has not issued, and it reads x 1: its R2. Writing now would destroy the value it must read. This is a WAR hazard, so write-back stalls.

150
00:23:49,226 --> 00:24:09,151
Store float, stf f4,Z(x1), issues in cycle 8. T1 and T2 of store are empty, so it reads f 4 and x 1 from the register file. The write-back in this cycle cleared its tag. A RAW-dependent issue can be in the same cycle.

151
00:24:09,601 --> 00:24:13,953
Load float in load still waits: its T2 is still A L U.

152
00:24:14,403 --> 00:24:34,329
Multiply float, mulf f4,f0,f2, dispatches in cycle 8. FP1 wrote f 4 in this cycle, so the register status of f 4 is empty now: no WAW hazard. FP2 has been free all the time, so mulf goes to FP2.

153
00:24:34,779 --> 00:24:44,827
Allocate the F P 2 entry. Busy is yes, op is multiply float. R is f 4, R1 is f 0, R2 is f 2.

154
00:24:45,277 --> 00:24:57,373
Copy the status of the inputs. The register status of f 0 is load, so T1 becomes load. F 2 has no tag, so T2 stays empty: f 2 is ready.

155
00:24:57,823 --> 00:25:06,079
Set the status of the output. F P 2 will write f 4, so the register status of f 4 becomes F P 2.

156
00:25:06,529 --> 00:25:10,838
Cycle 9. The tables show the state at the end of cycle 8.

157
00:25:11,288 --> 00:25:27,800
Add immediate, addi x1,x1,#4, writes back in cycle 9. The older store has issued now: it read x 1 in cycle 8. So the WAR hazard is gone. addi writes x 1 into the register file.

158
00:25:28,250 --> 00:25:35,482
The register status of x 1 holds A L U. Clear it: x 1 is now ready in the register file.

159
00:25:35,932 --> 00:25:46,087
Compare the tag A L U with T1 and T2 of the waiting entries. T2 of load matches. Clear it: x 1 is now ready for load.

160
00:25:46,537 --> 00:25:49,694
Free the A L U entry. Busy becomes no.

161
00:25:50,144 --> 00:26:09,600
Load float, ldf f0,X(x1), issues in cycle 9. T1 and T2 of load are empty, so it reads x 1 from the register file. The write-back in this cycle cleared its tag. A RAW-dependent issue can be in the same cycle.

162
00:26:10,050 --> 00:26:20,375
Multiply float in F P 2 cannot issue. Its T1 is load: f 0 is not ready. This is a RAW hazard, so it waits.

163
00:26:20,825 --> 00:26:23,556
Store float executes in cycle 9.

164
00:26:24,006 --> 00:26:34,566
stf f4,Z(x1) cannot dispatch. The store unit is busy with the first store. This is a structural hazard, so dispatch stalls.

165
00:26:35,016 --> 00:26:39,389
Cycle 10. The tables show the state at the end of cycle 9.

166
00:26:39,839 --> 00:26:52,938
Store float, stf f4,Z(x1), writes back in cycle 10. A store has no destination register, so there is no WAR check. The store unit finishes.

167
00:26:53,388 --> 00:26:56,225
Free the store entry. Busy becomes no.

168
00:26:56,675 --> 00:27:01,497
Multiply float in F P 2 still waits: its T1 is still load.

169
00:27:01,947 --> 00:27:04,549
Load float executes in cycle 10.

170
00:27:04,999 --> 00:27:18,717
Store float, stf f4,Z(x1), dispatches in cycle 10. The store unit was freed by the write-back in this cycle. A dispatch that waited for it can be in the same cycle.

171
00:27:19,167 --> 00:27:26,740
Allocate the store entry. Busy is yes, op is store float. R1 is f 4, R2 is x 1.

172
00:27:27,190 --> 00:27:40,630
Copy the status of the inputs. The register status of f 4 is F P 2, so T1 becomes F P 2. X 1 has no tag, so T2 stays empty: x 1 is ready.

173
00:27:41,080 --> 00:27:58,701
At the end of cycle 10, look at the order. addi is instruction 4, but it issued in cycle 5, executed in cycle 6 and wrote back in cycle 9. The older store, instruction 3, did each of these later. This is out-of-order execution.

174
00:27:59,151 --> 00:28:10,309
Here are the same results as a timing picture. Each row is an instruction, and each column is a cycle. The boxes show DS, IS, EX and WB.

175
00:28:10,759 --> 00:28:19,143
Look at addi, instruction 4. It issues in cycle 5, executes in cycle 6, and writes back in cycle 9.

176
00:28:19,593 --> 00:28:26,035
The store, instruction 3, is older. It issues in cycle 8 and writes back in cycle 10.

177
00:28:26,485 --> 00:28:31,691
So the younger addi passed the older store. The slide calls this out-of-orderness.

178
00:28:32,141 --> 00:28:41,762
This slide is optional. It is not on the slides. With the same rules, we can finish the two iterations, in cycles 11 to 17.

179
00:28:42,212 --> 00:28:50,852
The second load executes in cycle 10 and writes back in cycle 11. No older instruction waits to read f 0.

180
00:28:51,302 --> 00:29:05,489
The second multiply waits for f 0. The write-back in cycle 11 clears its tag, so it issues in cycle 11. It executes in cycles 12 to 14 and writes back in cycle 15.

181
00:29:05,939 --> 00:29:15,325
The second store waits for f 4. It issues in cycle 15, executes in cycle 16 and writes back in cycle 17.

182
00:29:15,775 --> 00:29:19,999
A summary of the scoreboard. The good side: the hardware is cheap.

183
00:29:20,449 --> 00:29:30,220
The performance is pretty good: a speedup of 1 point 7 times for FORTRAN programs, and 2 point 5 times for hand-coded assembly.

184
00:29:30,670 --> 00:29:43,257
The bad side: there is no bypassing. RAW dependencies go through the registers. In the example, the store waited until the multiply wrote f 4 into the register file.

185
00:29:43,707 --> 00:29:57,616
The scheduling scope is limited. WAW and structural hazards force dispatch to stay in order. In the example, the second multiply waited for the WAW hazard on f 4.

186
00:29:58,066 --> 00:30:10,930
WAR hazards delay the write-back, and so the issue of dependent operations. In the example, addi waited for the store to read x 1, and the second load waited for addi.

187
00:30:11,380 --> 00:30:29,471
Now assignment 7, question 1. The machine has 2 floating-point multiply units, which take 7 cycles, and 2 floating-point add units, which take 4 cycles. Show the scoreboard when FADD.D has finished executing, but has not written its result.

188
00:30:29,921 --> 00:30:40,993
There is a RAW hazard on F0. FSUB.D reads F0, and FMUL.D writes it. So FSUB.D must wait for FMUL.D.

189
00:30:41,443 --> 00:30:52,835
There is a WAR hazard on F2. FADD.D writes F2, and the older FSUB.D reads F2. FSUB.D must read the old F2 first.

190
00:30:53,285 --> 00:31:00,282
So for RAW, the reader waits for the writer. For WAR, the writer waits until the reader issues.

191
00:31:00,732 --> 00:31:16,945
If the scoreboard could not tell the two apart, FMUL.D could wait for FSUB.D, while FSUB.D waits for FMUL.D. This is a deadlock. Pause the video and try it: find the cycle of the snapshot, and fill the three tables.

192
00:31:17,395 --> 00:31:21,129
FMUL.D dispatches in cycle 1 to Mult1.

193
00:31:21,579 --> 00:31:26,571
Its sources F6 and F4 are ready, so it issues in cycle 2.

194
00:31:27,021 --> 00:31:30,967
It executes for 7 cycles: cycles 3 to 9.

195
00:31:31,417 --> 00:31:39,588
FSUB.D dispatches in cycle 2 to Add1. T1 is Mult1, because Mult1 will write F0.

196
00:31:40,038 --> 00:31:46,139
FADD.D dispatches in cycle 3 to Add2. F10 and F2 have no tags.

197
00:31:46,589 --> 00:31:49,213
So FADD.D issues in cycle 4.

198
00:31:49,663 --> 00:32:00,245
It executes for 4 cycles: cycles 5 to 8. At the end of cycle 8, FADD.D has finished executing. This is the cycle of the snapshot.

199
00:32:00,695 --> 00:32:08,567
FMUL.D writes back in cycle 10. No older instruction waits to read F0, so it does not wait.

200
00:32:09,017 --> 00:32:18,723
The write-back clears T1 of FSUB.D in cycle 10. So FSUB.D issues in cycle 10, and reads F0 and F2.

201
00:32:19,173 --> 00:32:34,768
FADD.D could write back in cycle 9. But the older FSUB.D has not read F2 yet. This is a WAR hazard. FSUB.D reads F2 in cycle 10, so FADD.D writes back in cycle 11.

202
00:32:35,218 --> 00:32:40,253
FSUB.D executes for 4 cycles: cycles 11 to 14.

203
00:32:40,703 --> 00:32:42,943
It writes back in cycle 15.

204
00:32:43,393 --> 00:33:02,785
Here is the scoreboard at the end of cycle 8. Black cycle numbers are done; blue ones come later. Mult1 holds FMUL.D: R is F0, R1 is F6, R2 is F4. No tags: its sources were ready. The register status of F0 is Mult1.

205
00:33:03,235 --> 00:33:15,224
Add1 holds FSUB.D: R is F8, R1 is F0, R2 is F2. T1 is Mult1. The register status of F8 is Add1.

206
00:33:15,674 --> 00:33:28,858
Add2 holds FADD.D: R is F2, R1 is F10, R2 is F2. No tags. The register status of F2 is Add2. The ALU, Mult2 and Div are not busy.

207
00:33:29,308 --> 00:33:44,220
How does the scoreboard tell RAW from WAR? A RAW hazard is a tag in T1 or T2. Add1 has T1 equal to Mult1, so FSUB.D waits for FMUL.D.

208
00:33:44,670 --> 00:34:00,947
A WAR hazard is an older instruction that has not issued and has the destination as a source. Add2 writes F2. Add1 is older, has not issued, and its R2 is F2. So FADD.D waits to write back.

209
00:34:01,397 --> 00:34:12,661
FMUL.D has no older instruction that waits to read F0. So it writes back in cycle 10 and does not wait for FSUB.D. There is no deadlock.

210
00:34:13,111 --> 00:34:22,903
Here is the recap. One. In an in-order pipeline, an instruction stalls behind an older stalled instruction, even when it does not depend on it.

211
00:34:23,353 --> 00:34:31,247
Two. Dynamic scheduling executes instructions out of order. But the result must look like sequential execution.

212
00:34:31,697 --> 00:34:39,334
Three. An instruction buffer splits decode into two stages. Dispatch is in order. Issue is out of order.

213
00:34:39,784 --> 00:34:51,261
Four. The scoreboard has three tables: instruction status, functional unit status with R, R1, R2, T1 and T2, and register status with T.

214
00:34:51,711 --> 00:35:05,919
Five. Dispatch stalls on structural and WAW hazards. Issue stalls on RAW hazards, when T1 or T2 is set. Write-back stalls on WAR hazards.

215
00:35:06,369 --> 00:35:17,399
Six. Write-back writes the register, clears the matching tags, and frees the entry. A dependent issue or dispatch can be in the same cycle as the write-back.

216
00:35:17,849 --> 00:35:34,489
Seven. The scoreboard is cheap and gives 1 point 7 to 2 point 5 times speedup. But it has no bypassing and a limited scope. Part 2 adds register renaming, with Tomasulo's algorithm. That is the end of video 20.
